 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>A Navy pilot's day on the radio runs from the squadron's base frequency to clearance, ground, tower, departure, the working area and back, mostly on preset UHF channels, with a short call sign that changes from flight to flight. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('19–35')}</a> Inside US airspace those calls follow the FAA's rules, so this module teaches both: the Navy flow first, then the FAA phraseology underneath it. Radio is the critical link between pilots and air traffic control (ATC). The FAA's Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM) asks pilots to keep transmissions brief, to acknowledge each ATC communication with the aircraft's call sign, and to prefer standard phraseology over jargon and slang. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-1')}</a> Good radio work is mostly listening: knowing what was said, to whom, and what you must do about it.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: follow a Navy training sortie's calls from the squadron base frequency to the break and back; use the training-rule calls that stop and start an engagement; transmit cleanly without blocking the frequency; spell with the phonetic alphabet; say altitudes, headings, speeds, frequencies and times the way the AIM does; make an initial call in the right order; choose the right acknowledgement word; read back the parts of a clearance that must be read back; follow a formation check-in and frequency push; make a distress call; change a transponder code without passing through an emergency code; and fly the AIM's lost-communications rules.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a substitute for your aircraft's NATOPS manual, your wing's and squadron's procedures, or the current AIM. The Navy examples come from one training squadron's scripts and training instructions; frequencies, channels, call signs and local calls differ by unit and base. Other services, ships and other countries use additional or different procedures. Carrier recovery communications are in the <a href={`${caseIii}#radio`}>Case III module's radio section</a>, which steps through a Case III approach call by call.</p>

 <h2 id="technique">Radio technique</h2>
 <p>The AIM's radio technique comes down to six habits. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-2')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Listen before you transmit.</strong> Much of what you need may come from ATIS or from monitoring the frequency. Transmitting over someone else's exchange is futile and will likely jam receivers, forcing everyone to repeat. After changing frequency, pause and listen to make sure it is clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Think before keying your transmitter.</strong> Know what you want to say; write a long message, such as an IFR position report, down first.</li>
  <li><strong>Press, pause, then speak.</strong> Hold the microphone close to your lips; a slight pause after pressing the button may be necessary to be sure the first word is transmitted. Speak in a normal, conversational tone.</li>
  <li><strong>Wait before calling again.</strong> After releasing the button, wait a few seconds before calling again: the controller may be writing down your call sign, looking for your flight plan, or working another frequency.</li>
  <li><strong>Listen to your receiver.</strong> Be alert to the sounds, or the lack of sounds, in your receiver; check the volume and frequency, and make sure your microphone is not stuck on transmit. A stuck microphone can block a frequency for long periods.</li>
  <li><strong>Be within range.</strong> Make sure you are within the performance range of your radio equipment and the ground station equipment. At some sites you can hear the station but it cannot hear you, and higher altitude extends VHF line-of-sight range.</li>
 </ol>
