 <h2 id="bands">Bands and emergency frequencies</h2>
 <p>Military air–ground voice uses the <strong>ultrahigh frequency (UHF)</strong> band, 300 to 3,000 MHz, which in some instances goes as low as 225 MHz. Civil air–ground voice uses the <strong>very high frequency (VHF)</strong> band, 30 to 300 MHz. <a href="#source-pcg">{g('VHF; UHF')}</a> A Navy jet usually carries both: in one training squadron's script the student works ATIS, clearance delivery, ground, tower, departure, approach and the squadron's own base frequency on UHF, and uses VHF to talk to a civil approach frequency and to cancel IFR. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('19–31')}</a></p>
 <p>Frequencies are set up in advance as numbered <strong>preset channels</strong>, so a controller or the brief can name a channel instead of a frequency: in that script UHF channel 4 is Sherman Tower, and approach hands the aircraft off with "contact Sherman Tower Button 4". <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('25, 32')}</a> Your squadron's channel card sets the numbers; the ones here are that script's.</p>
 <p>Two emergency frequencies matter to every pilot: <strong>121.5 MHz</strong> (VHF) and <strong>243.0 MHz</strong> (UHF). Both are generally limited to line of sight. 121.5 is guarded by direction-finding stations and some military and civil aircraft; 243.0 is guarded by military aircraft, so a military pilot in trouble has a UHF emergency frequency that other military aircraft are listening to. The frequency in use, or another ATC frequency, is still preferable for an emergency call. <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-3-1')}</a> 122.2 MHz is assigned to most flight service stations as a common en route simplex frequency. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-14')}</a></p>
