 <h2 id="alphabet">The phonetic alphabet</h2>
 <p>Use the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) phonetic alphabet when communications conditions make letters hard to tell apart, when aircraft with similar call signs share a frequency, and to spell difficult words. Each letter has a telephony word and a pronunciation; the digits have their own pronunciations, such as TREE, FIFE and NIN-ER. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-7')}</a></p>
 <div class="phonetic" role="region" aria-label="Phonetic alphabet table" tabindex="0">
  <table>
   <caption>Phonetic alphabet and Morse code <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-7')}</a></caption>
   <thead><tr><th scope="col">Character</th><th scope="col">Telephony</th><th scope="col">Pronunciation</th><th scope="col">Morse</th></tr></thead>
   <tbody>{PHONETIC.map(p => <tr><th scope="row">{p.char}</th><td>{p.word}</td><td>{p.say}</td><td class="morse">{morse(p.morse)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
 </div>

 <h2 id="numbers">Numbers on the radio</h2>
 <p>The AIM's rules for numbers: <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-8–4-2-12')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Hundreds and thousands.</strong> Round hundreds and thousands up to 9,900 are spoken as words ("four thousand five hundred"). Above 9,900, the digits before "thousand" are spoken separately ("one zero thousand"). Airway and jet route numbers are spoken as letters and words (V12 is "Victor Twelve"). Other numbers are spoken digit by digit. A decimal point is "point"; ICAO uses "decimal", and the FAA accepts it from pilots required to use it.</li>
  <li><strong>Altitudes.</strong> Below 18,000 ft mean sea level (MSL), say the thousands and hundreds: "one two thousand five hundred". At and above 18,000 ft (flight level 180), say "flight level" and three digits.</li>
  <li><strong>Directions.</strong> Bearings, courses, headings and wind directions are three digits and magnetic; add "true" when it applies.</li>
  <li><strong>Speeds.</strong> Digit by digit, then "knots". Mach numbers: "Mach" and the digits with "point".</li>
  <li><strong>Time.</strong> Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) on the 24-hour clock, digit by digit; "Zulu" may be used to denote UTC.</li>
 </ul>
