 </section>

 <h2 id="callsigns">Call signs</h2>
 <p><strong>Military call signs.</strong> The AIM only says that military aircraft use a variety of call sign systems. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-4')}</a> In Navy training the call sign is usually a squadron word and a number: the script's student is "KATT 11" for one flight and is told to use a different call sign for every practice event. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('20')}</a> The squadron answers on its own base frequency as "KATT Base", and the side number painted on the jet is passed separately, so the call sign names the flight, not the airframe: "KATT Base, KATT 11 taxi outbound, side number 000". <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('20')}</a> A formation has one tactical call sign for the whole flight, "Angry" in one squadron's examples, and each aircraft answers with its position in the flight, "Angry 1-2". <a href="#source-formsup">{f('2–3')}</a></p>
 <p>A military call sign is said as written: words, then the digits. The rest of this section is the FAA's rule set, which applies to everyone on a US civil frequency. Never abbreviate a call sign on initial contact, or when another aircraft on the frequency has a similar one. ATC may start abbreviating a call sign; after that the pilot may use the abbreviation, but ATC will not abbreviate the call signs of air carriers or other civil aircraft with authorized call signs. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-4')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Civil aircraft:</strong> the aircraft type, model or manufacturer's name, then the registration characters. When the type is stated, the "N" prefix is dropped: "Aztec Two Four Six Four Alfa". An abbreviated call sign is the prefix and the last three characters. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-4')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Air carriers</strong> use their authorized call sign and the flight number in group form, "United Two Twenty-Two", adding "Heavy" or "Super" when appropriate. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-3, 4-2-4')}</a></li>
    <li><strong>Student pilots</strong> can identify themselves as students on initial contact for extra help from ATC. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-4')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Ground stations are called by the facility's name followed by its type, such as "Sherman Ground" at a naval air station or "New York Radio" for a civil flight service station. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-6')}</a> <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('21')}</a></p>
