 <h2 id="sortie">A Navy training sortie, call by call</h2>
 <p>Training Squadron TEN (VT-10) gives its students a "Hollywood Script": every checklist and radio call of a T-6A training flight from Sherman Field, Naval Air Station Pensacola, in order. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('1, 19–35')}</a> It shows the shape of a Navy sortie on the radio:</p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Copy ATIS, then get the clearance.</strong> ATIS on UHF 1, then clearance delivery on UHF 2: "Sherman Clearance, KATT 11, IFR, NPA 630, on request, ready to copy." The clearance is read back, and clearance delivery answers "KATT 11, readback correct." <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('19–20')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Tell the squadron.</strong> On the squadron base frequency (UHF 19): "KATT Base, KATT 11 taxi outbound, side number 000"; before takeoff, "KATT Base, KATT 11 going flying." <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('20, 25')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Ground, then tower.</strong> "Sherman Ground, KATT 11, taxi, information (ATIS letter)"; later, on UHF 4, "Sherman Tower, KATT 11, takeoff IFR." The takeoff readback carries the runway and ends "switching": "KATT 11, cleared for takeoff Runway 7 right, switching." <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('21, 25')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Departure and the working area.</strong> Check in climbing with the altitude you are passing and the altitude you are climbing to. Departure clears the flight to its working area, a military operations area (MOA), with "frequency change approved"; the MOA common frequency that follows is a safety-of-flight frequency used by departure, so there is no check-in. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('27–29')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Come home on course rules.</strong> Cancel IFR when done in the area, copy ATIS, then "Pensacola Approach, KATT 11, 3500, information Bravo, request course rules." Approach hands the aircraft to tower at the entry point: "contact Sherman Tower Button 4." <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('30–32')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Break, land, and check back in.</strong> "Sherman Tower, KATT 11, Point Golf for the break"; "numbers 7 right"; tower: "right break approved". In the pattern, the position call ends with "gear, stop", and tower clears the aircraft to land. Stay with tower until clear of the runway, taxi back with ground, and finish on base: "KATT Base, KATT 11 in and up." <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('32–35')}</a></li>
 </ol>
 <p>Notice the readbacks: the script puts the call sign first ("KATT 11, cleared to land 7 Right"). The AIM asks for the call sign in every readback and its examples put it at either end, so both satisfy the FAA rule. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('34')}</a> <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-4-7')}</a></p>
