 <h2 id="contact">Making contact</h2>
 <p>An initial call has up to five parts, in this order: <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-3')}</a></p>
 <ol class="call-order">
  <li>The name of the facility you are calling.</li>
  <li>Your full aircraft identification, as filed in the flight plan.</li>
  <li>Your position, when you are on the airport surface.</li>
  <li>The type of message to follow, or your request if it is short.</li>
  <li>"Over", if required.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>The Navy script's calls follow the same order: "Sherman Ground, KATT 11, taxi, information (ATIS letter)" is facility, identification and request, with the ATIS code at the end. <a href="#source-hollywood">{h('21')}</a> The AIM's examples are "New York Radio, Mooney Three One One Echo" and "Columbia Ground, Cessna Three One Six Zero Foxtrot, south ramp, I-F-R Memphis". After contact is established, use the same format, combining the call-up and the message in one transmission. Acknowledge every call-up or clearance unless told otherwise, and acknowledge a frequency change: "United Two Twenty-Two on one three four point five". Change to the new frequency as soon as possible unless told to change at a time, fix or altitude. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-2-3')}</a> If the airport has automatic terminal information service (ATIS), say you have it on initial contact, using its code word: "have information Sierra". <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-1-13')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="call" aria-labelledby="call-title">
  <h3 id="call-title" class="fig-title">Figure 5 — Build the initial call</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <p>Select the pieces in the order you would say them.</p>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-choices></div>
  <ol class="built" data-built aria-label="Your call so far"></ol>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-check>Check the call</button><button type="button" data-undo>Undo</button><button type="button" data-next>Next situation</button></div>
