 </section>

 <h2 id="readback">Acknowledgement and readback</h2>
 <p>Include the aircraft identification in every readback and acknowledgement. Read back the parts of ATC clearances and instructions that contain altitude assignments, vectors or runway assignments, as a means of mutual verification, and read altitudes, altitude restrictions and vectors back in the order they were given. Do not read back charted procedures unless the controller specifically states them. An initial readback of a taxi, departure or landing clearance should include the runway. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-4-7')}</a> The AIM's examples: "Climbing to Flight Level three three zero, United Twelve" and "November Five Charlie Tango, roger, cleared to land runway nine left". <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-4-7')}</a> On an instrument flight rules (IFR) flight, make a written record of your clearance. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-4-7')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="readback" aria-labelledby="rb-title">
  <h3 id="rb-title" class="fig-title">Figure 6 — Read back the clearance</h3>
  <p>The controller's transmission is split into its parts. Select every part you must read back, then check.</p>
  <blockquote class="atc" data-clearance></blockquote>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-parts></div>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-check>Check readback</button><button type="button" data-next>Next instruction</button></div>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. For "KATT 11, wind 060 at 8, Runway 7 right, cleared for takeoff, switch to Departure", read back the runway with your call sign: "KATT 11, cleared for takeoff Runway 7 right, switching."</p>
  <p class="fig-caption">The checker requires the parts AIM 4-4-7 lists: identification, altitude assignments and restrictions, vectors, and runway assignments. Reading back more is not marked wrong, but is shown. The first two instructions and their readbacks are from the VT-10 script; the third and fifth are adapted from the AIM's examples; the others are constructed.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="flight">Flight check-ins and frequency changes</h2>
 <p>A formation talks to ATC with one voice, its lead, and uses a separate tactical frequency between its own aircraft. One US Navy training squadron's formation supplement, VT-6's, sets out the pattern. Lead checks the flight in on the briefed tactical frequency on the ground, and once the flight is up on that frequency, neither aircraft switches off it for any reason. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('2')}</a> Radio changes are of three kinds: pre-briefed automatic switches, ATC-directed changes and lead-directed changes. Switches and check-ins are never made on an ATC, area common or common traffic advisory frequency, to keep traffic off saturated frequencies. If unsure of the correct frequency, use plain, concise language on the tactical frequency to get well. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('2')}</a></p>
 <p>The flight's tactical call sign ("Angry" in the supplement's examples) addresses the whole flight; each aircraft answers with its position in the flight. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('2–3')}</a> Before taxi, lead calls "Angry, check Victor" and the wingman answers with its call sign and the ATIS code, or "Two, standby (X) mikes" if not ready. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('3')}</a> To leave the working area, lead calls "Angry, push UHF TAC" on the VHF tactical frequency, then checks the flight in on the new frequency: lead "Angry", wingman "Angry 1-2". A push to a frequency that becomes the working frequency, such as a VHF airfield frequency, has no check-in. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('10')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="flight" aria-labelledby="flight-title">
  <h3 id="flight-title" class="fig-title">Figure 7 — Check-in and push, call by call</h3>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-prev>Previous call</button><button type="button" data-next>Next call</button></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 7, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Lead and wingman radios, the tactical frequencies and who is transmitting"></svg></div>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Calls in order: on VHF TAC, "Angry, check Victor" – "Angry 1-2, with (ATIS code)"; later, "Angry, push UHF TAC"; both switch; on UHF TAC, "Angry" – "Angry 1-2".</p>
  <p class="fig-caption">One squadron's training example, simplified to the calls cited. Unit procedures, frequencies and call signs differ.</p>
 </section>
 <p>If a wingman loses its radio in formation, it gets lead's attention and signals by hand: patting the oxygen mask means it cannot talk, patting the ear of the helmet means it cannot hear, each followed by a thumbs-down. <a href="#source-formsup">{f('6')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="training">Training-rule calls</h2>
 <p>Air combat training adds a small set of calls whose job is safety: they stop, start and deconflict an engagement, and every player must know them before the first set. These come from the Navy's T-45 basic fighter maneuvering (BFM) instruction, CNATRA P-1289. The training rules "shall be strictly observed", and they start with 500 feet of separation between all aircraft at all times. <a href="#source-p1289">{b('3-1–3-2')}</a></p>
 <dl class="jargon">{TRAINING_CALLS.map(t => <div><dt>{t.call}</dt><dd>{t.meaning} <a href="#source-p1289">{b(t.page)}</a></dd></div>)}</dl>
 <p>"Knock it off" ends each set as well as any unsafe one: the instructor (dual) or bandit (solo) calls it at the end of every set, and both aircraft acknowledge. <a href="#source-p1289">{b('2-4')}</a> Passing head-on, the pass is called so both pilots go the same way: "high", "low", "right-to-right" or "left-to-left". <a href="#source-p1289">{b('3-4')}</a> The maneuvers themselves are in the <a href={bfm}>BFM module</a>.</p>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="training" aria-labelledby="training-title">
  <h3 id="training-title">Quiz: which training call?</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
