 </section>

 <h2 id="emergency">Distress and urgency</h2>
 <p>Distress communications have absolute priority over all others; urgency communications come next. A distress call begins with <strong>MAYDAY</strong>, preferably said three times; an urgency call uses <strong>PAN-PAN</strong> in the same way. <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-3-1')}</a> Make the first call on the frequency in use, and do not change frequency or ground station unless absolutely necessary. <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-3-1, 6-3-2')}</a> Transmit as many of these items as you need, preferably in this order: <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-3-2')}</a></p>
 <ol class="call-order distress">{DISTRESS_ORDER.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ol>
 <p>While you are in radio contact, keep the assigned transponder code unless ATC tells you otherwise. If you cannot immediately reach an ATC facility, squawk code 7700 with altitude reporting (Mode C). <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-3-2')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="transponder">Transponder codes</h2>
 <p>A transponder answers radar with a four-digit code. Unless ATC assigns one, a visual flight rules (VFR) aircraft squawks <strong>1200</strong>. Three codes are special: <strong>7500</strong> is the hijack code, <strong>7600</strong> signals two-way radio failure, and <strong>7700</strong> signals an emergency; 7600 and 7700 trigger special indications in automated facilities. When changing codes routinely, avoid passing through 7500, 7600 or 7700: to change from 2700 to 7200, first select 2200, then 7200. <a href="#source-aim">{a('4-1-20')}</a></p>
