 <h3>In instrument conditions</h3>
 <p>If two-way radio communication fails in VFR conditions, or VFR conditions are met after the failure, continue the flight under VFR and land as soon as practicable. In IFR conditions, fly the route and altitude below. <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-4-1')}</a> Squawk 7600, and remember that you may not be in radar coverage. <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-4-2')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Route</strong>, the first of these that applies: the route assigned in the last ATC clearance received; if being radar vectored, direct from the point of radio failure to the fix, route or airway specified in the vector clearance; without an assigned route, the route ATC has advised may be expected in a further clearance; otherwise, the route filed in the flight plan.</li>
  <li><strong>Altitude</strong>, for the route segment being flown, the highest of: the altitude or flight level assigned in the last ATC clearance received; the minimum altitude for IFR operations; and the altitude or flight level ATC has advised may be expected in a further clearance, once you reach the time or fix at which ATC said to expect it.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaving the clearance limit.</strong> If the limit is a fix from which an approach begins, start descent or descent and approach as close as possible to the expect-further-clearance time if you received one; if not, as close as possible to the estimated time of arrival from the filed or amended estimated time en route. If the limit is not an approach fix, leave it at the expect-further-clearance time if you received one, or on arrival if not, and proceed to a fix from which an approach begins; then start descent or descent and approach as close as possible to the estimated time of arrival calculated from the filed or amended estimated time en route.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="fig-caption">All three rules: <a href="#source-aim">{a('6-4-1')}</a></p>
