 </section>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>You lose sight of your wingman during an engagement. What do you say and do?</summary><p>"(Call sign) blind", and turn away from the predicted collision bearing. "No joy" is for a threat aircraft you cannot see, not your wingman.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Approach tells you "contact Sherman Tower Button 4". What is Button 4?</summary><p>A preset radio channel, here channel 4, which the squadron's channel card sets to Sherman Tower.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Approach asks "Can you accept a visual approach?" and you can. Is "Roger" the answer?</summary><p>No. Roger only says you received the transmission and must not answer a yes-or-no question. Say "Affirmative" with your call sign.</p></details>
 <details><summary>How do you say 17,500 ft and 18,500 ft?</summary><p>"One seven thousand five hundred". At and above 18,000 ft it becomes a flight level: "flight level one eight five".</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are told "turn right heading zero niner zero, descend and maintain three thousand, altimeter two niner niner two". What must you read back?</summary><p>The heading and the altitude, in that order, with your call sign. The AIM's readback list does not include the altimeter setting.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You need to go from 1700 to 7100. Which knob first?</summary><p>The second digit: 1700 → 1100 → 7100. Setting the first digit first would send 7700.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Your radio fails in cloud while you are being vectored. Which route?</summary><p>Direct from the point of failure to the fix, route or airway in the vector clearance.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A steady red light from the tower while you are in the pattern?</summary><p>Give way to other aircraft and continue circling.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Where do you make a MAYDAY call?</summary><p>On the frequency in use first; 121.5 or 243.0 if needed. Do not change frequency unless absolutely necessary.</p></details>

